format fuses the two lines into one word: it prints

    The quick brown foxjumps over the lazy dog.

The soft break between "fox" and "jumps" is dropped, and nothing is put in its
place, so the last word of the first line runs straight into the first word of
the second. This is data loss in prose, not a layout difference, and it hits
every paragraph in every document that is wrapped at a column.

What it should do: keep the line break (or, if it means to reflow, replace it
with a single space). Either answer reads correctly; the current one does not.

Cause: MarkdownParser.getChildrenText concatenates the text of each inline
child. For a CMARK_NODE_SOFTBREAK child, getNodeText takes the
cmark_render_commonmark output — a newline — and then calls
trimmingCharacters(in: .whitespacesAndNewlines) on it, which turns it into the
empty string. The same trim inside getNodeText will empty any inline node
whose whole rendering is whitespace.
